.Dd August 27, 2026
.Dt SNIDUMP 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm snidump
.Nd extract TLS SNI and HTTP Host headers from network traffic
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl hpqtj
.Op Fl f Ar bpf
.Op Fl c Ar N
.Fl i Ar interface
.Op Fl w Ar dump.pcap
.Nm
.Op Fl hpqtj
.Op Fl f Ar bpf
.Op Fl c Ar N
.Fl r Ar trace.pcap
.Op Fl w Ar dump.pcap
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
captures network traffic and extracts hostnames from two sources:
.Bl -bullet -compact
.It
The
.Em server_name
extension (SNI) of TLS ClientHello handshake messages
.Pq RFC 4366 .
.It
The
.Em Host
request-header field of plaintext HTTP/1.1 and HTTP/1.0 requests
.Pq RFC 2616 .
.El
.Pp
Input is either a live network interface or a previously recorded PCAP file.
Both IPv4 and IPv6 are supported.
IPv6 extension headers (Hop-by-Hop, Routing, Destination Options, Fragment,
Authentication Header) are walked transparently to locate the transport layer.
.Pp
Exactly one of
.Fl i
or
.Fl r
must be supplied.
.Sh OPTIONS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl i Ar interface
Capture live traffic from
.Ar interface .
Requires
.Sy CAP_NET_RAW
on Linux or root on FreeBSD.
.It Fl r Ar trace.pcap
Read packets from
.Ar trace.pcap
instead of a live interface.
No special privileges are required.
.It Fl f Ar bpf
Override the default BPF capture filter with
.Ar bpf .
See
.Sx DEFAULT BPF FILTER
and
.Xr pcap-filter 7
for syntax.
.It Fl p
Enable promiscuous mode.
Only meaningful with
.Fl i .
.It Fl w Ar dump.pcap
Write all packets that match the BPF filter to
.Ar dump.pcap
in standard pcap format.
.It Fl q
Quiet mode.
Suppress all informational output
.Pq PID, interface, BPF string, statistics, etc. .
Only hostname events are written to stdout.
Recommended when the output is piped to another program or redirected to a log file.
.It Fl t
Prefix each output line with a UTC timestamp in ISO\ 8601 format
.Pq Sy YYYY-MM-DDThh:mm:ssZ .
.It Fl j
Emit one JSON object per line instead of plain text
.Pq NDJSON / JSON\ Lines format .
Each object includes the timestamp, protocol, source endpoint, destination
endpoint, and hostname.
When an HTTP
.Em Host
header carries an explicit port
.Pq e.g.\& Sy Host:\ example.com:8080 ,
a
.Sy port
field is added to the JSON object.
This flag implies timestamped output.
.It Fl c Ar N
Stop after
.Ar N
hostname matches and exit cleanly.
Useful for one-shot scripting or quick tests.
.It Fl h
Print a usage summary to stderr and exit.
.El
.Sh OUTPUT FORMATS
.Ss Plain text (default)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
<src-addr>:<src-port> -> <dst-addr>:[<dst-port>] <len>:<hostname>
.Ed
.Pp
IPv4 example:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
192.168.1.10:52001 -> 140.82.121.4:[443] 14:www.github.com
.Ed
.Pp
IPv6 addresses are enclosed in brackets to delimit them from the port:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[2001:db8::1]:52001 -> [2001:4860::1]:[443] 14:www.google.com
.Ed
.Pp
The destination port appears in square brackets.
The length prefix is the byte-length of the SNI value (for TLS) or the
Host header value (for HTTP), measured after stripping any explicit port.
.Pp
Informational messages and error diagnostics are written to stderr.
Hostname events are always written to stdout.
.Ss With Fl t No (timestamp)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
2026-08-27T14:23:01Z 192.168.1.10:52001 -> 140.82.121.4:[443] 14:www.github.com
.Ed
.Ss With Fl j No (JSON)
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"time":"2026-08-27T14:23:01Z","proto":"TLS",
 "src":"192.168.1.10:52001","dst":"140.82.121.4:443",
 "host":"www.github.com"}
{"time":"2026-08-27T14:23:02Z","proto":"HTTP",
 "src":"192.168.1.10:52200","dst":"93.184.216.34:8080",
 "host":"example.com","port":8080}
.Ed
.Pp
Fields:
.Bl -tag -width "proto" -compact
.It Sy time
UTC timestamp in ISO\ 8601 format.
.It Sy proto
.Sq TLS
or
.Sq HTTP .
.It Sy src , dst
Endpoint in
.Sq address:port
form.
IPv6 addresses are bracketed.
.It Sy host
The extracted hostname.
Any
.Sq :port
suffix present in an HTTP
.Em Host
header is stripped from this field.
.It Sy port
.Em Optional.
Present only when an HTTP
.Em Host
header contains an explicit port number
.Pq e.g.\& Sy Host:\ example.com:8080 .
Never present for TLS SNI entries.
.El
.Sh DEFAULT BPF FILTER
.Bd -literal -offset indent
(ip or ip6) and tcp and
(tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-push == tcp-push) and
(dst port 80 or dst port 443)
.Ed
.Pp
This targets TCP segments with the PSH flag set going to port 80 (HTTP)
or 443 (HTTPS/TLS) on both IPv4 and IPv6.
.Pp
The PSH flag restriction is intentional.
.Nm
does not perform TCP stream reassembly; a TLS ClientHello fragmented across
multiple segments cannot be parsed regardless of which segments are admitted.
Filtering to PSH-flagged segments (which almost always mark a complete
application-layer write) reduces noise without missing anything the tool
could successfully parse.
.Pp
Override with
.Fl f
when capturing on non-standard ports or when UDP is required.
.Sh BUILDING
.Nm
depends on
.Sy libpcap
and
.Sy libpcre2 .
.Pp
On Debian / Ubuntu:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo apt install libpcap-dev libpcre2-dev
make
.Ed
.Pp
On FreeBSD and pfSense
.Pq which is FreeBSD-based ,
.Sy libpcap
is part of the base system.
.Sy pcre2
must be installed from ports.
The Makefile defaults to
.Sy gcc ;
use
.Sy clang
and supply the
.Pa /usr/local
prefix where ports install headers and libraries:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo pkg install pcre2
make CC=clang CFLAGS="-I/usr/local/include" LDFLAGS="-L/usr/local/lib"
.Ed
.Sh BINARIES
Two binaries are built:
.Bl -tag -width snidump_noether
.It Nm snidump
Standard build.
Expects Ethernet frames
.Pq Dv DLT_EN10MB .
Handles both IPv4 and IPv6.
.It Nm snidump_noether
Built with
.Dv __NO_ETHERNET__
defined.
Skips the Ethernet header.
Use on tunnel or raw-IP interfaces
.Pq e.g.\& Sy tun0 , gif0 .
Detects IPv4 vs IPv6 from the version nibble of each raw IP frame.
.El
.Pp
.Nm
checks the datalink type reported by libpcap at startup and exits with a
fatal error if it does not match the compiled-in expectation, preventing
silent misparse on interfaces with unexpected link-layer framing.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Live capture on
.Pa eth0 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo snidump -i eth0
.Ed
.Pp
Promiscuous capture with JSON output and no informational messages:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo snidump -pqj -i eth0
.Ed
.Pp
Read from a PCAP file, extract sorted unique hostnames:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
snidump -r capture.pcap | awk '{print $NF}' | cut -d: -f2- | sort -u
.Ed
.Pp
Read from a PCAP file with JSON, extract unique hostnames using
.Xr jq 1 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
snidump -qjr capture.pcap | jq -r .host | sort -u
.Ed
.Pp
Capture TLS on a non-standard port:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo snidump -i eth0 \e
  -f '(ip or ip6) and tcp and \e
      (tcp[tcpflags] & tcp-push == tcp-push) and dst port 8443'
.Ed
.Pp
Stop after the first 10 matches
.Pq useful for quick tests or one-shot scripts :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo snidump -c 10 -i eth0
.Ed
.Pp
Capture one TLS handshake from a PCAP file and print it as JSON:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
snidump -qjc 1 -r capture.pcap
.Ed
.Pp
The
.Sy port
field appears in JSON output when an HTTP
.Em Host
header carries an explicit port, e.g.\&
.Sy Host:\ example.com:8080 :
.Bd -literal -offset indent
{"time":"...","proto":"HTTP","src":"...","dst":"...","host":"example.com","port":8080}
.Ed
.Pp
Tunnel interface (no Ethernet header):
.Bd -literal -offset indent
sudo snidump_noether -i tun0
.Ed
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width contrib/snidump.logrotate
.It Pa contrib/snidump.service
Sample systemd unit file.
Runs
.Nm
as an unprivileged user with only
.Sy CAP_NET_RAW .
.It Pa contrib/snidump.logrotate
Sample logrotate configuration for
.Pa /var/log/snidump/hosts.jsonl .
.It Pa contrib/snidump.rc
Sample FreeBSD rc.d script using
.Xr daemon 8
for daemonization and automatic restart.
.El
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
.Nm
exits\ 0 on success (including clean shutdown via
.Dv SIGINT
or
.Dv SIGTERM )
and non-zero on fatal error.
Fatal errors are reported to stderr with a
.Sq [FATAL]
prefix.
Non-fatal warnings carry a
.Sq [WARNING]
prefix and do not cause
.Nm
to exit.
.Sh LIMITATIONS
.Bl -bullet
.It
.Sy "No TCP stream reassembly."
Each packet payload is parsed independently.
A TLS ClientHello spread across multiple TCP segments will not be matched.
In practice this is rare on standard-MTU links.
.It
.Sy IPv4 only for IP-level operations.
The transport parsers (TLS, HTTP) are protocol-agnostic, but IPv6 extension
header types
.Dv ESP
(encrypted) and unrecognised next-header values cause the packet to be
silently skipped.
.It
.Sy QUIC / HTTP3 not supported.
QUIC encapsulates the TLS ClientHello inside its own framing and requires
decryption of the Initial packet (using a publicly known derived key) before
the SNI is accessible.
.It
.Sy HTTP/1.0 and HTTP/1.1 only.
HTTP/2 hostnames are captured via the TLS SNI mechanism.
HTTP/3 is not supported (see QUIC above).
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr pcap 3 ,
.Xr pcap-filter 7 ,
.Xr tcpdump 8 ,
.Xr jq 1
